    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d319">

                <pb facs="0490" n="391" xml:id="pg391"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.56241/57</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Emmet</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 583</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">The
                                Hague</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1936-11-12"
                            >November 12, 1936</date>.<lb/>[Received November 24.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s Instruction No. 269, of June 23, 1936, upon receipt of which a note
                    was sent to the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, (No. 288, of July 2, 1936), a copy
                    of which is enclosed.</p>
                <p>A satisfactory reply, a copy and translation of which are enclosed, has just been
                    received from the Minister of Foreign Affairs, from which it will be seen that,
                    on a reciprocal basis, the Netherlands Government agrees to our suggestions.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grenville T.
                    Emmet</hi></signed></closer>




